NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"issue-1975-alias-scope-identity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Issue #1975: alias scope identity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"18abceee4"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin/main"},{"type":"text","value":") plus the working-tree repair on\nbranch "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"red/issue-1975-materialize-alias"},{"type":"text","value":". Owner:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/query/includes-oracle.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includes alpha-renaming across sibling scopes"},{"type":"text","value":"), with grammar, model,\ndriver, and observation in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/query/includes-scope-identity-oracle.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". Alias\nvalidation is owned by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/query/validate-aliases.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"defect-and-cause","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defect and cause"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A live query returned no rows when an include reused an alias from a joined\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"from()"},{"type":"text","value":" subquery that received a pushed-down predicate. The optimizer copied,\nwrapped, or collapsed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CollectionRef"},{"type":"text","value":" objects with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"new CollectionRef(...)"},{"type":"text","value":",\nwhich mints a fresh "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SourceId"},{"type":"text","value":". Before #1877 the compiler compiled the user's\noriginal subquery and discarded the optimized copy, so the orphaned IDs were\nnever read. #1877 compiles the optimized copy when it differs. The compiler\nthen missed every orphaned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SourceId"},{"type":"text","value":" and fell back to alias text.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bindSourceInputs"},{"type":"text","value":" had written every scope's input under its alias, so the\nlookup returned a sibling scope's source with the same name."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"repair","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repair"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimizer.ts"},{"type":"text","value":": the four copy, wrap, and collapse sites reuse the existing\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CollectionRef"},{"type":"text","value":" (lines near 500, 848, 935, and 945)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compiler/index.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compiler/joins.ts"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bindSourceInputs"},{"type":"text","value":" maps\ncaller-supplied alias keys to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SourceId"},{"type":"text","value":"s once and no longer publishes\nalias keys; both source lookups read "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allInputs[sourceId]"},{"type":"text","value":" only. A lost\nidentity now raises "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CollectionInputNotFoundError"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compiler/index.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validateQueryStructure"},{"type":"text","value":": no scope inside an include\ncan reuse an alias its ancestors can see, including a subquery alias. This\ncovers the include itself, its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unionAll()"},{"type":"text","value":" branches, and its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"from()"},{"type":"text","value":" and\njoin subqueries, which can read the parent row through their callbacks.\nThe builder previously accepted these namings and returned wrong rows.\nTop-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"from()"},{"type":"text","value":" subqueries see no ancestor, so they may still reuse names."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compiler/index.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validateQueryStructure"},{"type":"text","value":": one query cannot give two of\nits sources the same alias. Two joins with one alias were accepted before."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An include sees its ancestors' from and join aliases, but not the aliases\ninside a parent's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unionAll()"},{"type":"text","value":" branches. A union row holds the branches'\nprojected fields, so those names belong to sibling scopes. An earlier\nrevision of this change rejected an include that reused a branch alias, which\nbase "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"18abceee4"},{"type":"text","value":" accepted with correct rows. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unionParent"},{"type":"text","value":" topology now\nguards that legal naming."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bug-class-boundary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bug-class boundary"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ARCHITECTURE.md normative law 1 and §Identity: renaming an\naccepted alias cannot change an explicitly projected result, and a plan\nrewrite preserves "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SourceId"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Histories:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the owner grammar (three topologies; zero to two joins;\nLEFT or INNER; zero to two predicates in separate or combined form; plain,\nordered-and-limited, or DISTINCT bodies; include form, source, and filter;\nouter spread or fields; eager or on-demand finite providers; up to four\nsource writes)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production path:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createLiveQueryCollection"},{"type":"text","value":" through optimizer,\ncompiler, and live builder."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observations:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" complete public rows after preload and after each write,\ncompared with recomputation and across namings; per-Collection\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" WHERE clauses for on-demand sources."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Original reproduction"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Issue query returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":" on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"18abceee4"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[1]"},{"type":"text","value":" with the repair. Bisected first bad commit: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"40a5aea56"},{"type":"text","value":" (#1877)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pinned witnesses"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All 13 behavioral witnesses (issue rows plus six topologies in eager and on-demand modes) fail on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"18abceee4"},{"type":"text","value":" and pass with the repair."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Campaigns"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1715"},{"type":"text","value":" and the random campaign (80 runs each) fail on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"18abceee4"},{"type":"text","value":" and pass with the repair."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optimizer site mutants"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reverting site 500, 848, 935, or 945 alone fails the owner (4, 13, 13, and 13 tests). The pure-wrapper witness is the only witness that reaches site 500."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identity-only binding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With it, each optimizer site mutant also fails the existing suite with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CollectionInputNotFoundError"},{"type":"text","value":" (3, 53, 146, and 59 tests) instead of returning wrong rows. With the optimizer repair in place it is behavior-equivalent; its value is converting a future identity loss into an error."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Include shadowing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Four rejection witnesses (include, nested include, include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unionAll()"},{"type":"text","value":" branch, include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"from()"},{"type":"text","value":" subquery) fail on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"18abceee4"},{"type":"text","value":" and pass with the repair; a sibling-reuse control passes on both."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generated rejection"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One in four scenarios draws any naming; illegal ones must be rejected, and shadowing ones with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DuplicateAliasInSubqueryError"},{"type":"text","value":". At a 10x budget this check found the duplicate-join acceptance, and it fails when include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"from()"},{"type":"text","value":" visibility or the same-scope check is reverted. Reverting union-branch visibility is caught only by its pinned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validate-aliases.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" witness, because the include level already checks branch aliases."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Union scopes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reintroducing branch aliases into an include's visible set fails both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unionParent"},{"type":"text","value":" witnesses and both campaigns at the default budget. A derived branch alias and a same-named union join return the same rows as a renamed join; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validate-aliases.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" pins both legal namings."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Planted model faults (ignore child filter, LEFT as INNER, drop join duplicates) fail only at \"canonical naming against recomputation\"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An alias-dependent WHERE routing mutant fails only at \"loadSubset requests per Collection\"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ablation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Restricting the earlier grammar to all-distinct names made both campaigns pass on the unrepaired code; the failure needs cross-scope reuse."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enumeration (earlier grammar)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Failures needed a joined subquery with a pushed source predicate and an include reusing the subquery source or join alias. Outer-alias collisions alone never failed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full suite"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm --filter @tanstack/db test"},{"type":"text","value":" passes."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"guide-conformance-orc-001-to-orc-014","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guide conformance (ORC-001 to ORC-014)"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Law, authority, and limits are in the owner's opening comment."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The recomputation model reads plain arrays and never consults\naliases, plans, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SourceId"},{"type":"text","value":"s."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The contract and campaigns are in the owner. The grammar,\nmodel, driver, and observation are in named sections of the companion."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Reconstruction: every pinned witness is a grammar member.\nAblation and enumeration are recorded above. Range: IDs 1–4, three parts,\nfour refs and notes, and a three-name pool chosen for frequent collisions.\nExclusion: the legality test rejects shadowing, same-scope reuse, and\nrepeated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unionAll()"},{"type":"text","value":" branch names."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Real live-query Collections are compared at named checkpoints.\nFailure messages carry the checkpoint, the write, and the naming."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Site mutants, a hostile routing mutant, and planted model\nfaults are each rejected at the intended checkpoint."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generatedCampaigns"},{"type":"text","value":" runs the same property with a fixed seed\nand without one. Replay with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PROPERTY=includes.scoped-alpha-renaming"},{"type":"text","value":"\nplus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_SEED"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PATH"},{"type":"text","value":". The property\nis registered in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"oracle-config.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"oracle-replay-manifest.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Not applicable: the model is a stateless recomputation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The companion's opening comment maps slots, roles, aliases, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SourceId"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Cleanup failures join the primary failure in an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregateError"},{"type":"text","value":" whose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cause"},{"type":"text","value":" is the violated law."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Shared-fault hypothesis: every naming of one shape could be\nwrong in the same way. The recomputation model is the second formulation\nthat distinguishes it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" This record."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The pinned wrapper witness distinguishes \"reuse references\nwhere the optimizer copies\" from \"reuse references except during\ncollapse\"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The on-demand provider is controlled. Its premise is a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" WHERE clause naming Collection fields. Real-provider\nreception of that premise belongs to the load-subset owners."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"open-in-scope-cells","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open in-scope cells"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These legal forms are in the bug class but outside the owner's grammar. Probes\nduring review passed on both revisions, so none is a known counterexample, but\nnone is protected by this owner:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"nested includes, and includes inside a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"from()"},{"type":"text","value":" subquery;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"join subqueries outside the wrapper topology, and joined subqueries with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"having"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"outer RIGHT and FULL joins;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"publication events, observer timing, and unload under reused names;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ordered windows and lazy join loading, whose lazy-target lookup reads\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aliasRemapping"},{"type":"text","value":", a map merged by alias across include scopes;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"temporal demand, cancellation, and progressive loading."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The identity-only binding makes a lost "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SourceId"},{"type":"text","value":" raise an error in any of\nthese forms. That is the current protection against silent wrong rows there."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"issue-1975-alias-scope-identity","text":"Issue #1975: alias scope identity","level":1},{"id":"defect-and-cause","text":"Defect and cause","level":2},{"id":"repair","text":"Repair","level":2},{"id":"bug-class-boundary","text":"Bug-class boundary","level":2},{"id":"evidence","text":"Evidence","level":2},{"id":"guide-conformance-orc-001-to-orc-014","text":"Guide conformance (ORC-001 to ORC-014)","level":2},{"id":"open-in-scope-cells","text":"Open in-scope cells","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Issue #1975: alias scope identity\n\nReviewed head: `18abceee4` (`origin/main`) plus the working-tree repair on\nbranch `red/issue-1975-materialize-alias`. Owner:\n`packages/db/tests/query/includes-oracle.property.test.ts`\n(`includes alpha-renaming across sibling scopes`), with grammar, model,\ndriver, and observation in\n`packages/db/tests/query/includes-scope-identity-oracle.ts`. Alias\nvalidation is owned by `packages/db/tests/query/validate-aliases.test.ts`.\n\n## Defect and cause\n\nA live query returned no rows when an include reused an alias from a joined\n`from()` subquery that received a pushed-down predicate. The optimizer copied,\nwrapped, or collapsed `CollectionRef` objects with `new CollectionRef(...)`,\nwhich mints a fresh `SourceId`. Before #1877 the compiler compiled the user's\noriginal subquery and discarded the optimized copy, so the orphaned IDs were\nnever read. #1877 compiles the optimized copy when it differs. The compiler\nthen missed every orphaned `SourceId` and fell back to alias text.\n`bindSourceInputs` had written every scope's input under its alias, so the\nlookup returned a sibling scope's source with the same name.\n\n## Repair\n\n- `optimizer.ts`: the four copy, wrap, and collapse sites reuse the existing\n  `CollectionRef` (lines near 500, 848, 935, and 945).\n- `compiler/index.ts` and `compiler/joins.ts`: `bindSourceInputs` maps\n  caller-supplied alias keys to `SourceId`s once and no longer publishes\n  alias keys; both source lookups read `allInputs[sourceId]` only. A lost\n  identity now raises `CollectionInputNotFoundError`.\n- `compiler/index.ts` `validateQueryStructure`: no scope inside an include\n  can reuse an alias its ancestors can see, including a subquery alias. This\n  covers the include itself, its `unionAll()` branches, and its `from()` and\n  join subqueries, which can read the parent row through their callbacks.\n  The builder previously accepted these namings and returned wrong rows.\n  Top-level `from()` subqueries see no ancestor, so they may still reuse names.\n- `compiler/index.ts` `validateQueryStructure`: one query cannot give two of\n  its sources the same alias. Two joins with one alias were accepted before.\n- An include sees its ancestors' from and join aliases, but not the aliases\n  inside a parent's `unionAll()` branches. A union row holds the branches'\n  projected fields, so those names belong to sibling scopes. An earlier\n  revision of this change rejected an include that reused a branch alias, which\n  base `18abceee4` accepted with correct rows. The `unionParent` topology now\n  guards that legal naming.\n\n## Bug-class boundary\n\n- **Contract:** ARCHITECTURE.md normative law 1 and §Identity: renaming an\n  accepted alias cannot change an explicitly projected result, and a plan\n  rewrite preserves `SourceId`.\n- **Histories:** the owner grammar (three topologies; zero to two joins;\n  LEFT or INNER; zero to two predicates in separate or combined form; plain,\n  ordered-and-limited, or DISTINCT bodies; include form, source, and filter;\n  outer spread or fields; eager or on-demand finite providers; up to four\n  source writes).\n- **Production path:** `createLiveQueryCollection` through optimizer,\n  compiler, and live builder.\n- **Observations:** complete public rows after preload and after each write,\n  compared with recomputation and across namings; per-Collection\n  `loadSubset` WHERE clauses for on-demand sources.\n\n## Evidence\n\n| Check                         | Result                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Original reproduction         | Issue query returns `[]` on `18abceee4`, `[1]` with the repair. Bisected first bad commit: `40a5aea56` (#1877).                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| Pinned witnesses              | All 13 behavioral witnesses (issue rows plus six topologies in eager and on-demand modes) fail on `18abceee4` and pass with the repair.                                                                                                                                                       |\n| Campaigns                     | Fixed seed `1715` and the random campaign (80 runs each) fail on `18abceee4` and pass with the repair.                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Optimizer site mutants        | Reverting site 500, 848, 935, or 945 alone fails the owner (4, 13, 13, and 13 tests). The pure-wrapper witness is the only witness that reaches site 500.                                                                                                                                     |\n| Identity-only binding         | With it, each optimizer site mutant also fails the existing suite with `CollectionInputNotFoundError` (3, 53, 146, and 59 tests) instead of returning wrong rows. With the optimizer repair in place it is behavior-equivalent; its value is converting a future identity loss into an error. |\n| Include shadowing             | Four rejection witnesses (include, nested include, include `unionAll()` branch, include `from()` subquery) fail on `18abceee4` and pass with the repair; a sibling-reuse control passes on both. |\n| Generated rejection | One in four scenarios draws any naming; illegal ones must be rejected, and shadowing ones with `DuplicateAliasInSubqueryError`. At a 10x budget this check found the duplicate-join acceptance, and it fails when include `from()` visibility or the same-scope check is reverted. Reverting union-branch visibility is caught only by its pinned `validate-aliases.test.ts` witness, because the include level already checks branch aliases. |\n| Union scopes | Reintroducing branch aliases into an include's visible set fails both `unionParent` witnesses and both campaigns at the default budget. A derived branch alias and a same-named union join return the same rows as a renamed join; `validate-aliases.test.ts` pins both legal namings. |\n| Model calibration             | Planted model faults (ignore child filter, LEFT as INNER, drop join duplicates) fail only at \"canonical naming against recomputation\".                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Request calibration           | An alias-dependent WHERE routing mutant fails only at \"loadSubset requests per Collection\".                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| Ablation                      | Restricting the earlier grammar to all-distinct names made both campaigns pass on the unrepaired code; the failure needs cross-scope reuse.                                                                                                                                                   |\n| Enumeration (earlier grammar) | Failures needed a joined subquery with a pushed source predicate and an include reusing the subquery source or join alias. Outer-alias collisions alone never failed.                                                                                                                         |\n| Full suite                    | `pnpm --filter @tanstack/db test` passes.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n\n## Guide conformance (ORC-001 to ORC-014)\n\n- **ORC-001:** Law, authority, and limits are in the owner's opening comment.\n- **ORC-002:** The recomputation model reads plain arrays and never consults\n  aliases, plans, or `SourceId`s.\n- **ORC-003:** The contract and campaigns are in the owner. The grammar,\n  model, driver, and observation are in named sections of the companion.\n- **ORC-004:** Reconstruction: every pinned witness is a grammar member.\n  Ablation and enumeration are recorded above. Range: IDs 1–4, three parts,\n  four refs and notes, and a three-name pool chosen for frequent collisions.\n  Exclusion: the legality test rejects shadowing, same-scope reuse, and\n  repeated `unionAll()` branch names.\n- **ORC-005:** Real live-query Collections are compared at named checkpoints.\n  Failure messages carry the checkpoint, the write, and the naming.\n- **ORC-006:** Site mutants, a hostile routing mutant, and planted model\n  faults are each rejected at the intended checkpoint.\n- **ORC-007:** `generatedCampaigns` runs the same property with a fixed seed\n  and without one. Replay with `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PROPERTY=includes.scoped-alpha-renaming`\n  plus `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_SEED` and `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PATH`. The property\n  is registered in `oracle-config.ts` and `oracle-replay-manifest.ts`.\n- **ORC-008:** Not applicable: the model is a stateless recomputation.\n- **ORC-009:** The companion's opening comment maps slots, roles, aliases, and\n  `SourceId`.\n- **ORC-010:** Cleanup failures join the primary failure in an\n  `AggregateError` whose `cause` is the violated law.\n- **ORC-011:** Shared-fault hypothesis: every naming of one shape could be\n  wrong in the same way. The recomputation model is the second formulation\n  that distinguishes it.\n- **ORC-012:** This record.\n- **ORC-013:** The pinned wrapper witness distinguishes \"reuse references\n  where the optimizer copies\" from \"reuse references except during\n  collapse\".\n- **ORC-014:** The on-demand provider is controlled. Its premise is a\n  `loadSubset` WHERE clause naming Collection fields. Real-provider\n  reception of that premise belongs to the load-subset owners.\n\n## Open in-scope cells\n\nThese legal forms are in the bug class but outside the owner's grammar. Probes\nduring review passed on both revisions, so none is a known counterexample, but\nnone is protected by this owner:\n\n- nested includes, and includes inside a `from()` subquery;\n- join subqueries outside the wrapper topology, and joined subqueries with\n  `groupBy` or `having`;\n- outer RIGHT and FULL joins;\n- publication events, observer timing, and unload under reused names;\n- ordered windows and lazy join loading, whose lazy-target lookup reads\n  `aliasRemapping`, a map merged by alias across include scopes;\n- temporal demand, cancellation, and progressive loading.\n\nThe identity-only binding makes a lost `SourceId` raise an error in any of\nthese forms. That is the current protection against silent wrong rows there.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Issue #1975: alias scope identity\n\nReviewed head: `18abceee4` (`origin/main`) plus the working-tree repair on\nbranch `red/issue-1975-materialize-alias`. Owner:\n`packages/db/tests/query/includes-oracle.property.test.ts`\n(`includes alpha-renaming across sibling scopes`), with grammar, model,\ndriver, and observation in\n`packages/db/tests/query/includes-scope-identity-oracle.ts`. Alias\nvalidation is owned by `packages/db/tests/query/validate-aliases.test.ts`.\n\n## Defect and cause\n\nA live query returned no rows when an include reused an alias from a joined\n`from()` subquery that received a pushed-down predicate. The optimizer copied,\nwrapped, or collapsed `CollectionRef` objects with `new CollectionRef(...)`,\nwhich mints a fresh `SourceId`. Before #1877 the compiler compiled the user's\noriginal subquery and discarded the optimized copy, so the orphaned IDs were\nnever read. #1877 compiles the optimized copy when it differs. The compiler\nthen missed every orphaned `SourceId` and fell back to alias text.\n`bindSourceInputs` had written every scope's input under its alias, so the\nlookup returned a sibling scope's source with the same name.\n\n## Repair\n\n- `optimizer.ts`: the four copy, wrap, and collapse sites reuse the existing\n  `CollectionRef` (lines near 500, 848, 935, and 945).\n- `compiler/index.ts` and `compiler/joins.ts`: `bindSourceInputs` maps\n  caller-supplied alias keys to `SourceId`s once and no longer publishes\n  alias keys; both source lookups read `allInputs[sourceId]` only. A lost\n  identity now raises `CollectionInputNotFoundError`.\n- `compiler/index.ts` `validateQueryStructure`: no scope inside an include\n  can reuse an alias its ancestors can see, including a subquery alias. This\n  covers the include itself, its `unionAll()` branches, and its `from()` and\n  join subqueries, which can read the parent row through their callbacks.\n  The builder previously accepted these namings and returned wrong rows.\n  Top-level `from()` subqueries see no ancestor, so they may still reuse names.\n- `compiler/index.ts` `validateQueryStructure`: one query cannot give two of\n  its sources the same alias. Two joins with one alias were accepted before.\n- An include sees its ancestors' from and join aliases, but not the aliases\n  inside a parent's `unionAll()` branches. A union row holds the branches'\n  projected fields, so those names belong to sibling scopes. An earlier\n  revision of this change rejected an include that reused a branch alias, which\n  base `18abceee4` accepted with correct rows. The `unionParent` topology now\n  guards that legal naming.\n\n## Bug-class boundary\n\n- **Contract:** ARCHITECTURE.md normative law 1 and §Identity: renaming an\n  accepted alias cannot change an explicitly projected result, and a plan\n  rewrite preserves `SourceId`.\n- **Histories:** the owner grammar (three topologies; zero to two joins;\n  LEFT or INNER; zero to two predicates in separate or combined form; plain,\n  ordered-and-limited, or DISTINCT bodies; include form, source, and filter;\n  outer spread or fields; eager or on-demand finite providers; up to four\n  source writes).\n- **Production path:** `createLiveQueryCollection` through optimizer,\n  compiler, and live builder.\n- **Observations:** complete public rows after preload and after each write,\n  compared with recomputation and across namings; per-Collection\n  `loadSubset` WHERE clauses for on-demand sources.\n\n## Evidence\n\n| Check                         | Result                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Original reproduction         | Issue query returns `[]` on `18abceee4`, `[1]` with the repair. Bisected first bad commit: `40a5aea56` (#1877).                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| Pinned witnesses              | All 13 behavioral witnesses (issue rows plus six topologies in eager and on-demand modes) fail on `18abceee4` and pass with the repair.                                                                                                                                                       |\n| Campaigns                     | Fixed seed `1715` and the random campaign (80 runs each) fail on `18abceee4` and pass with the repair.                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Optimizer site mutants        | Reverting site 500, 848, 935, or 945 alone fails the owner (4, 13, 13, and 13 tests). The pure-wrapper witness is the only witness that reaches site 500.                                                                                                                                     |\n| Identity-only binding         | With it, each optimizer site mutant also fails the existing suite with `CollectionInputNotFoundError` (3, 53, 146, and 59 tests) instead of returning wrong rows. With the optimizer repair in place it is behavior-equivalent; its value is converting a future identity loss into an error. |\n| Include shadowing             | Four rejection witnesses (include, nested include, include `unionAll()` branch, include `from()` subquery) fail on `18abceee4` and pass with the repair; a sibling-reuse control passes on both. |\n| Generated rejection | One in four scenarios draws any naming; illegal ones must be rejected, and shadowing ones with `DuplicateAliasInSubqueryError`. At a 10x budget this check found the duplicate-join acceptance, and it fails when include `from()` visibility or the same-scope check is reverted. Reverting union-branch visibility is caught only by its pinned `validate-aliases.test.ts` witness, because the include level already checks branch aliases. |\n| Union scopes | Reintroducing branch aliases into an include's visible set fails both `unionParent` witnesses and both campaigns at the default budget. A derived branch alias and a same-named union join return the same rows as a renamed join; `validate-aliases.test.ts` pins both legal namings. |\n| Model calibration             | Planted model faults (ignore child filter, LEFT as INNER, drop join duplicates) fail only at \"canonical naming against recomputation\".                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Request calibration           | An alias-dependent WHERE routing mutant fails only at \"loadSubset requests per Collection\".                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| Ablation                      | Restricting the earlier grammar to all-distinct names made both campaigns pass on the unrepaired code; the failure needs cross-scope reuse.                                                                                                                                                   |\n| Enumeration (earlier grammar) | Failures needed a joined subquery with a pushed source predicate and an include reusing the subquery source or join alias. Outer-alias collisions alone never failed.                                                                                                                         |\n| Full suite                    | `pnpm --filter @tanstack/db test` passes.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n\n## Guide conformance (ORC-001 to ORC-014)\n\n- **ORC-001:** Law, authority, and limits are in the owner's opening comment.\n- **ORC-002:** The recomputation model reads plain arrays and never consults\n  aliases, plans, or `SourceId`s.\n- **ORC-003:** The contract and campaigns are in the owner. The grammar,\n  model, driver, and observation are in named sections of the companion.\n- **ORC-004:** Reconstruction: every pinned witness is a grammar member.\n  Ablation and enumeration are recorded above. Range: IDs 1–4, three parts,\n  four refs and notes, and a three-name pool chosen for frequent collisions.\n  Exclusion: the legality test rejects shadowing, same-scope reuse, and\n  repeated `unionAll()` branch names.\n- **ORC-005:** Real live-query Collections are compared at named checkpoints.\n  Failure messages carry the checkpoint, the write, and the naming.\n- **ORC-006:** Site mutants, a hostile routing mutant, and planted model\n  faults are each rejected at the intended checkpoint.\n- **ORC-007:** `generatedCampaigns` runs the same property with a fixed seed\n  and without one. Replay with `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PROPERTY=includes.scoped-alpha-renaming`\n  plus `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_SEED` and `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PATH`. The property\n  is registered in `oracle-config.ts` and `oracle-replay-manifest.ts`.\n- **ORC-008:** Not applicable: the model is a stateless recomputation.\n- **ORC-009:** The companion's opening comment maps slots, roles, aliases, and\n  `SourceId`.\n- **ORC-010:** Cleanup failures join the primary failure in an\n  `AggregateError` whose `cause` is the violated law.\n- **ORC-011:** Shared-fault hypothesis: every naming of one shape could be\n  wrong in the same way. The recomputation model is the second formulation\n  that distinguishes it.\n- **ORC-012:** This record.\n- **ORC-013:** The pinned wrapper witness distinguishes \"reuse references\n  where the optimizer copies\" from \"reuse references except during\n  collapse\".\n- **ORC-014:** The on-demand provider is controlled. Its premise is a\n  `loadSubset` WHERE clause naming Collection fields. Real-provider\n  reception of that premise belongs to the load-subset owners.\n\n## Open in-scope cells\n\nThese legal forms are in the bug class but outside the owner's grammar. Probes\nduring review passed on both revisions, so none is a known counterexample, but\nnone is protected by this owner:\n\n- nested includes, and includes inside a `from()` subquery;\n- join subqueries outside the wrapper topology, and joined subqueries with\n  `groupBy` or `having`;\n- outer RIGHT and FULL joins;\n- publication events, observer timing, and unload under reused names;\n- ordered windows and lazy join loading, whose lazy-target lookup reads\n  `aliasRemapping`, a map merged by alias across include scopes;\n- temporal demand, cancellation, and progressive loading.\n\nThe identity-only binding makes a lost `SourceId` raise an error in any of\nthese forms. That is the current protection against silent wrong rows there.\n"}
